Find a file whose name is stored as the Mac wrote it.

A name arrives from the client decomposed, which is how macOS writes them, and
mtoupath() composes it before anything touches the disk: CH_UTF8_MAC carries
CHARSET_DECOMPOSED, so the conversion to the volume charset takes the
precompose branch every time. That is right for a volume holding composed
names and wrong for one holding the decomposed form, where the composed name
is not there at all.

What the client sees is a listing it cannot act on. Finder shows the file,
asks for it by the name it was shown, gets ENOENT, and reports that the item
is in use -- so the folder holding it can never be emptied or deleted.
Measured on a 247 GB NTFS volume: 4 of 28 entries were stored decomposed and
every one of them was unreachable, while the same names created through AFP
worked, because those had been composed on the way in.

The composed name is what is tried, and a volume holding composed names never
reaches the second look. A miss that says the name is not there at all gives
the client's own bytes their turn, and when those are not there either the
composed name is put back: a create request must still make the composed name,
or new files would start being stored in whichever form the client sent.

--- a/etc/afpd/directory.c	2026-09-12 16:45:24
+++ b/etc/afpd/directory.c	2026-09-12 16:45:24
@@ -1641,7 +1641,42 @@
  * 16. chdir into the dir and
  * 17. set m_name to the mac equivalent of "."
  * 18. goto 3
